---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { src } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Heating fuel price publisher: who runs Heat Fuel Prices';
const description = 'Heat Fuel Prices is published by Radif Partners from the federal weekly survey of heating oil and propane prices: no dealer listed, no ZIP code or phone asked.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Heat Fuel Prices</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>The gap this site fills</h2>
    <p>Every Wednesday from October to March, the federal government publishes what households pay for heating oil and propane in 38 states. The tables are accurate and dry: prices to the tenth of a cent, without taxes, without the cost of a delivery, without a word on whether this winter is cheap or dear. The pages that rank for "heating oil prices" or "propane prices" are mostly dealer directories, lead forms that want your ZIP code and phone number, or copies of the federal table with the arithmetic done wrong. A household with a tank in the basement or in the yard wants three answers: what will the next fill cost, what will the whole winter cost, and should it sign the fixed-price contract its dealer is offering.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public rules and public data. It is not a fuel dealer, a propane supplier, a broker or a lead generator. It is not affiliated with the Energy Information Administration, NYSERDA, any state energy office or any assistance program, receives no commission and has no partner to send you to. Pages carry no affiliate link and no dealer pays to appear.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we rely on</h2>
    <p>The EIA's State Heating Oil and Propane Program (<Fragment set:html={src('eia_shopp', 'eia.gov')} />), its Winter Fuels Outlook (<Fragment set:html={src('eia_wfo', 'Table WF01')} />), NYSERDA's regional prices for New York (<Fragment set:html={src('nyserda_ho', 'data.ny.gov')} />), NOAA's degree day normals, the Census Bureau's count of homes by heating fuel, and the federal LIHEAP rules and income tables. Source: U.S. Energy Information Administration, for every weekly price and forecast shown.</p>
    <h2>What the site leaves out</h2>
    <p>It does not quote your dealer's price, which can sit well above or below the state average, and it does not know your local taxes. Winter budgets rest on an average home and normal weather. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every step and its limits; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for corrections and sources. We answer questions about a figure or a rule, not requests for a dealer.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
